          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"This is a direct first-person avowal presenting Lorck's love for Constance as true. His immediate insistence that it is serious reinforces the truth-claiming force, regardless of sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression itself avows a present emotional state and does not undertake an obligation or commitment. Lorck separately offers himself, his life, and his soul immediately afterward; that neighbouring offer should not be transferred onto “Jeg elsker dig.” Constance rejects the offer with “Nej, uf nej.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Nej, uf nej\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although Lorck is visibly agitated and emotionally intense, the focal words form a deliberate, intelligible avowal that he explicitly characterizes as serious. The evidence does not present them as a reflex-like cry or affective discharge beyond that assertion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Stemmen var saa indtrængende\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately represented as an avowal of love under T. Its courtship and persuasive role in the scene does not establish a distinct core mode beyond T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lorck declares his love directly to Constance during an unwanted confession. He has already said that he has carried an infatuation for over a year and then identifies her as its object. He kneels, presses her to yield to him, and later repeats the avowal while offering himself, his life, and his soul. Constance resists, tells him to stop, and rejects his offer.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal words themselves contain no undertaking. In the adjacent statement, Lorck offers Constance “mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl” and asks whether she will accept it. The precise practical terms are unspecified, but it functions as a relational offer by Lorck. Constance explicitly rejects it with “Nej, uf nej” and asks him to leave.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text explicitly identifies the work as Amalie Skram's Constance Ring; I also recognise that title and author. The assessment relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The passages strongly support that Lorck experiences infatuation or passion, but they do not independently settle the depth, durability, or sincerity of what he calls love. His coercive and insulting conduct bears on the character of the encounter but does not remove the avowal's T force.\"}"
